-component-type=3D"RichTextContainer" style=3D"transform:none"><p dir=3D"au=
to" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8"><em class=3D"framer-=
text">By Dr. Cara Touretzky, Dr. Robert Luce, and Dr. David Torres Sanchez<=
/em></p><h4 dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1vbjtdw"=
 id=3D"why-pay-attention-to-hardware-and-algorithm-choice">Why Pay Attentio=
n to Hardware and Algorithm Choice? </h4><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-te=
xt framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">For those of us who like to focus on the <=
!--$--><a class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-aqyyvd" href=3D"https:/=
/www.gurobi.com/resources/blog/optimization-modeling-the-art-of-not-making-=
it-an-art" target=3D"_blank">art of modeling</a><!--/$-->, it=E2=80=99s eas=
y to take for granted the influence of different hardware options on optimi=
zation performance. And with a reliable solver like Gurobi, modelers don=E2=
=80=99t have to spend time contemplating the algorithms being deployed unde=
r the hood.  </p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-=
1mhmov8">But hardware options have been evolving, and so have the algorithm=
s used in the optimization space. While the simplex and barrier methods for=
 solving LPs are well established (and continuing to improve each year), th=
e Primal-Dual Hybrid Gradient method (PDHG, also known as PDLP when <!--$--=
><a class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-aqyyvd" href=3D"https://proce=
edings.neurips.cc/paper/2021/file/a8fbbd3b11424ce032ba813493d95ad7-Paper.pd=
f" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"">enhanced and adapted specifically to Linear P=
rogramming</a><!--/$-->) has recently received significant attention becaus=
e it is amenable to a GPU-based implementation.  </p><p dir=3D"auto" class=
=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">With new GPU-enabled algorith=
ms for barrier and PDHG, the average optimization user benefits from unders=
tanding the impact of hardware and algorithm choice when deploying optimiza=
tion models=E2=80=94especially if they are interested in finding the combin=
ation that results in the fastest possible solution times.  </p><p dir=3D"a=
uto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">Spoiler alert: ther=
e is no silver bullet when it comes to hardware and algorithm selection. Th=
e classic advice of =E2=80=9Cit depends on your model=E2=80=9D still applie=
s. At Gurobi, we have done a thorough comparison of these algorithms and ha=
rdware options. We want to share our methodology and industry-proven benchm=
ark results with the optimization community to help navigate these choices.=
  </p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">Th=
is article will demystify the applicable algorithms and reveal new opportun=
ities for the field of optimization. Specifically, we have observed that gi=
ant LPs (and possibly MIPs that rely on giant LP relaxations) show the most=
 immediate promise for experiencing a significant speed boost from GPU-enab=
led LP algorithms.  </p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-=
preset-1mhmov8">Gurobi has a proven track record of taking new innovations =
in the optimization space and turning them into practical tools for industr=
y applications. Historically, this has been achieved through close collabor=
ation with Gurobi customers, who are pushing the frontier of our solver=E2=
=80=99s capabilities. Now that Gurobi has established a benchmark methodolo=
gy for comparing the various hardware and algorithm choices, we are ready a=
nd waiting to test your real-world models. Please reach out through <!--$--=
><a class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-aqyyvd" href=3D"https://suppo=
rt.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/articles/360050236512-How-do-I-get-support-for-using=
-Gurobi" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"">How do I get support for using Gurobi? =
</a><!--/$-->if you are interested in testing your models=E2=80=99 performa=
nce with a GPU-accelerated solver.  </p><h4 dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-te=
xt framer-styles-preset-1vbjtdw" id=3D"demystifying-the-optimization-and-gp=
u-space">Demystifying the Optimization and GPU Space </h4><p dir=3D"auto" c=
lass=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">We have investigated mult=
iple setups for solving giant LPs, with a combination of new hardware and a=
lgorithm options. </p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-pr=
eset-1mhmov8">Hardware options we=E2=80=99ve investigated: </p><ol dir=3D"a=
uto" class=3D"framer-text" style=3D"--max-list-digits:1"><li data-preset-ta=
g=3D"p" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8"><p class=3D"fram=
er-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">CPU 1: The CPU capabilities of the Gr=
ace Hopper Superchip 200 (GH200) with 72 cores</p></li><li data-preset-tag=
=3D"p" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8"><p class=3D"frame=
r-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">CPU 2: AMD=E2=80=99s EPYC 9655 with 96=
 cores</p></li><li data-preset-tag=3D"p" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles=
-preset-1mhmov8"><p class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">GPU:=
 The GPU capabilities of the GH200 chip</p></li></ol><p dir=3D"auto" class=
=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">For the CPU options, all benc=
hmarks are run using 64 cores, rather than the total available. The additio=
nal benefit of using more than 64 cores on either machine was diminishing, =
and using the same number of cores for all tests enabled a simple compariso=
n.  </p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">=
Gurobi=E2=80=99s algorithmic options for giant LPs are (1) the barrier algo=
rithm, and (2) PDHG, a first-order method. These two algorithms differ sign=
ificantly in their computational footprint. We=E2=80=99ll summarize the mos=
t important properties: </p><ol dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text" style=3D=
"--max-list-digits:1"><li data-preset-tag=3D"p" class=3D"framer-text framer=
-styles-preset-1mhmov8"><p class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov=
8">For the barrier algorithm, the heaviest computational step in each itera=
tion is the solution of a large, sparse system of linear equations. On the =
GPU, we are using NVIDIA=E2=80=99s cuDSS library to perform this operation,=
 while on the CPU we=E2=80=99re using our standard, in-house, multi-threade=
d algorithm.</p></li><li data-preset-tag=3D"p" class=3D"framer-text framer-=
styles-preset-1mhmov8"><p class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8=
">In PDHG, the most significant algorithmic operation per iteration is the =
multiplication of a sparse matrix (or its transpose) with a dense vector. O=
n the GPU, we are using NVIDIA=E2=80=99s cuSPARSE library to execute this o=
peration and keep all data resident in the GPU memory, so that no expensive=
 memory transfers are needed. On the CPU, such matrix vector products are c=
omputed using our standard, in-house, multi-threaded algorithm. </p></li></=
ol><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">If yo=
u=E2=80=99re wondering why the primal and dual simplex methods are not part=
 of this discussion, it is because these algorithms are already difficult t=
o parallelize on CPU hardware, and even more so on the GPU. </p><h4 dir=3D"=
auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1vbjtdw" id=3D"a-comment-on=
-convergence-tolerance-and-crossover">A Comment on Convergence Tolerance an=
d Crossover </h4><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-=
1mhmov8">Convergence behavior is different for the main LP algorithms. Barr=
ier methods have the benefit of displaying locally quadratic convergence, s=
o once the method gets close to a solution, it is very quick to shrink the =
residual to a small value. On the other hand, PDHG displays linear behavior=
 at best, so it can take longer to reach a very low tolerance threshold.  <=
/p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">The t=
olerance that we use to define convergence can vary, but industry practices=
 seem to generally agree that residuals less than 1e-6 are acceptable for a=
 quality solution. What=E2=80=99s the risk of increasing the convergence to=
lerance when you are trying to get a solution as fast as possible? To answe=
r that question, we have to talk about crossover and how it can add extra t=
ime to improve the result of the barrier and PDHG algorithms.  </p><p dir=
=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8"><!--$--><a cla=
ss=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-aqyyvd" href=3D"https://docs.gurobi.=
com/projects/optimizer/en/current/reference/parameters.html#parametercrosso=
ver" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"">Crossover</a><!--/$--> is an essential but =
often forgotten step needed when using a non-simplex method for LPs. To sum=
marize, crossover helps translate the LP solution into a =E2=80=9Cclean bas=
ic solution.=E2=80=9D Solutions from an interior point algorithm (or first-=
order method) normally have noisy values close to zero (we can call this de=
nse) and, more importantly, due to these, they may have violations in vario=
us metrics (such as primal feasibility, dual feasibility, and complementary=
 slackness). Crossover is very effective at cleaning up these violations an=
d providing a clean and sparse basic solution. </p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D=
"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">Most of our customers regard a b=
asic solution as essential to interpretability. Therefore, when evaluating =
these new algorithms, we feel it is necessary to also account for the time =
needed to complete crossover. The importance of this will be made even clea=
rer in the results below, because for some algorithms, there is a clear tra=
deoff between accuracy and the time needed to complete crossover. </p><h4 d=
ir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1vbjtdw" id=3D"comput=
ational-results">Computational Results </h4><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer=
-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">Of course, one would hope that the =E2=
=80=9Cbest=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cfastest=E2=80=9D machine would solve all op=
timization problems equally fast. In practice, however, one can observe sig=
nificant variation across the different hardware options and the two algori=
thms mentioned above.  </p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styl=
es-preset-1mhmov8">We give two insightful examples from the MIPLIB public l=
ibrary of models, and will conclude with a comment on the results from our =
internal set of benchmarks. The MIPLIB examples were selected because they =
show the possible variation in solution times due to the combination of mod=
el, algorithm, and hardware selection. For a more in-depth analysis of thes=
e results and additional examples, see <!--$--><a class=3D"framer-text fram=
er-styles-preset-aqyyvd" href=3D"https://www.gurobi.com/resources/webinar-e=
vents/new-options-for-solving-giant-lps" target=3D"_blank">New Options for =
Solving Giant LPs - Gurobi Optimization</a><!--/$-->.</p><p dir=3D"auto" cl=
ass=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8"><strong class=3D"framer-te=
xt">Key takeaways from our experiments: </strong></p><ul dir=3D"auto" class=
=3D"framer-text"><li data-preset-tag=3D"p" class=3D"framer-text framer-styl=
es-preset-1mhmov8"><p class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">GP=
Us typically perform PDHG and barrier iterations much faster than CPUs, but=
 that doesn=E2=80=99t always translate directly into big overall reductions=
 in runtime. </p></li><li data-preset-tag=3D"p" class=3D"framer-text framer=
-styles-preset-1mhmov8"><p class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov=
8">PDHG may be the algorithm of choice for some models, even on a CPU.  </p=
></li><li data-preset-tag=3D"p" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1=
mhmov8"><p class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">There are sti=
ll cases where barrier is the algorithm of choice, even on the CPU. The imp=
rovement from running barrier on GPU can be significant, but it is not as c=
onsistent as PDHG. </p></li><li data-preset-tag=3D"p" class=3D"framer-text =
framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8"><p class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-=
1mhmov8">Increasing convergence tolerance makes things faster, especially f=
or PDHG=E2=80=94but crossover may dominate the total time to achieve a qual=
ity solution. That is a new tradeoff for practitioners.  </p></li></ul><p d=
ir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8"><strong clas=
s=3D"framer-text">Example Model 1:  </strong></p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"f=
ramer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">The first model (=E2=80=9Crwth-tim=
etable=E2=80=9D) is an LP relaxation of a time tabling problem with a lot o=
f rows and columns that result in high computational effort in the barrier =
algorithm. The statistics are as follows: </p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"fram=
er-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8"><strong class=3D"framer-text">rwth-ti=
metable: 440,134 rows, 923,564 columns and 4,510,786 nonzeros </strong></p>=
<p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">We visua=
lize the comparison of the different hardware and algorithmic options expla=
ined above by means of a bar plot:  </p><img alt=3D"" width=3D"512" height=
=3D"238" src=3D"https://framerusercontent.com/images/RRYw8eiKHdFeTTzWQ1guND=
A2A.png" class=3D"framer-text framer-image framer-styles-preset-14xv0ao" st=
yle=3D"aspect-ratio:1024 / 477"><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer=
-styles-preset-1mhmov8">Each bar of the plot corresponds to a different har=
dware and algorithm combination, noting that PDHG has several columns, each=
 with different relative tolerance termination criteria. Each bar is split =
into two colors: time spent performing iterations in the respective algorit=
hm (red), and the time spent in crossover (green). From the hardware listed=
 above, the first four bars correspond to CPU 1, the following bar correspo=
nds to CPU 2, and the last two bars correspond to GPU.  </p><p dir=3D"auto"=
 class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">In this first example, =
we can see that PDHG with a low convergence tolerance (1e-6) is the clear w=
inner, regardless of the hardware, CPU 1 or GPU. As shown by the last two b=
ars, both algorithms running on the GPU bring some improvement to their CPU=
 counterparts. Specifically, for PDHG on the GPU, we see an improvement of =
62.71% over CPU 1. For barrier on the GPU, we see a 50% improvement over CP=
U 1, and only a modest 7% improvement over CPU 2.  </p><p dir=3D"auto" clas=
s=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">Another interesting takeaway=
 is that PDHG with higher tolerances, as expected, spends less time perform=
ing iterations=E2=80=94but unfortunately, this doesn=E2=80=99t help the tot=
al solution time, as crossover takes a large proportion of this. This is be=
cause crossover largely benefits from higher-quality solutions. </p><p dir=
=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8"><strong class=
=3D"framer-text">Example Model 2: </strong></p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"fra=
mer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">The second problem is the LP relaxat=
ion of an open-pit mining problem (=E2=80=9Crmine25=E2=80=9D) that has a hu=
ge number of rows: </p><img alt=3D"" width=3D"512" height=3D"213" src=3D"ht=
tps://framerusercontent.com/images/00FUl0EKK5XbitG9ppUhmlzX4Q.png" class=3D=
"framer-text framer-image framer-styles-preset-14xv0ao" style=3D"aspect-rat=
io:1024 / 426"><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1m=
hmov8"><strong class=3D"framer-text">rmine25: 2,953,849 rows, 326,599 colum=
ns, 7,182,744 nonzeros </strong></p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text fr=
amer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">In the rmine25 results, we can see that barrier=
 is the algorithm of choice across all hardware options, with an even furth=
er advantage when using the fastest CPU 2. Both CPU 2 and the GPU version f=
or barrier show a speedup nearly 40% over CPU 1. Interestingly, the time sp=
ent in crossover dominates the PDHG runtime, even for a convergence toleran=
ce as small as 1e-6.</p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-=
preset-1mhmov8">With results like this, it is hard to predict which algorit=
hm and hardware choice is the best for a given model, without even mentioni=
ng the effect of termination tolerance on crossover. Put differently, it is=
 safe to say there is no one-size-fits-all combination that is set to win! =
And we haven=E2=80=99t even factored into this discussion a range of other =
interesting phenomena: For example, although on these two models the barrie=
r runtime of CPU 2 and GPU are largely comparable, we=E2=80=99ve seen that =
the actual throughput for the factorization that cuDSS is doing can be much=
 larger than the throughput we achieve on the CPU. </p><h4 dir=3D"auto" cla=
ss=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1vbjtdw" id=3D"our-research-continue=
s">Our Research Continues</h4><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-s=
tyles-preset-1mhmov8">Whether you identify as an =E2=80=9Coptimization pers=
on=E2=80=9D who wanted to learn more about your hardware options, or a =E2=
=80=9Chigh-performance computing person=E2=80=9D who wanted to learn more a=
bout optimization, we hope this article guided you to the same place of app=
reciation for the latest initiatives in the field of GPU-enabled optimizati=
on algorithms.  </p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-pres=
et-1mhmov8">As was stated earlier, there is no silver bullet when it comes =
to hardware and algorithm selection for optimization. We still recommend tr=
eating every model as a unique creation that must be tested to see which co=
mbination is the best fit. The two examples shown earlier reflect the impac=
t that these choices can have, given the currently available state-of-the-a=
rt hardware options.  </p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-style=
s-preset-1mhmov8">Because the hardware and related libraries (recall, we le=
veraged NVIDIA=E2=80=99s cuDSS and cuSparse libraries for our GPU-enabled a=
lgorithms) are continuously evolving, we expect the results of our benchmar=
k to change rapidly=E2=80=94and it=E2=80=99s possible many more use cases w=
ill benefit from barrier and PDHG on GPU in the future.  </p><p dir=3D"auto=
" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">At this point you may =
be wondering why we are only sharing results for models from the MIPLIB dat=
abase when Gurobi has many <!--$--><a class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-pr=
eset-aqyyvd" href=3D"https://www.gurobi.com/resources/case-studies" target=
=3D"_blank">real-world case studies</a><!--/$-->. Our preliminary and inter=
nal benchmarks show only 72 out of 2633 models where PDHG is better than ou=
r default settings (only 17 of those show more than a factor of 2 improveme=
nt). But by nature of our collection, this set is highly biased towards LPs=
 that we can already solve with either Simplex or IPMs. Why does our test s=
et not include giant LPs that we cannot solve? This is more of a philosophi=
cal question, because why would someone write a giant LP if they expect tha=
t current solver technology cannot handle it?  </p><p dir=3D"auto" class=3D=
"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">This is an active research area,=
 and perception of what is =E2=80=9Cgiant=E2=80=9D is always shifting. With=
 the new GPU-enabled algorithms, our initial impression is that they will o=
ffer the most benefit to models so large that people have not yet dared to =
build them. Of course, we=E2=80=99re also exploring models that may not be =
=E2=80=9Cgiant,=E2=80=9D but have a level of complexity and heavy compute n=
eeds that will warrant using the new algorithms and GPU hardware.</p><p dir=
=3D"auto" class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-1mhmov8">Interested in =
qualifying your model for GPU-accelerated optimization? Please reach out th=
rough <!--$--><a class=3D"framer-text framer-styles-preset-aqyyvd" href=3D"=
https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/articles/360050236512-How-do-I-get-supp=
ort-for-using-Gurobi" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"">How do I get support for u=
sing Gurobi? </a><!--/$--></p></div></div></section><!--$--><div class=3D"f=
